{"id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:warped-product-constant-one","title":"prop:warped-product-constant-one","path":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.md","line":496,"body":"","kind":"theorem","status":"not_started","tex":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex#prop:warped-product-constant-one","uses":["[[references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:warped-product]]","[[references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:product-metric]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex","label":"prop:warped-product-constant-one","line":816,"source":"\\begin{proposition}[Constant warping gives the product metric]\n\\label{prop:warped-product-constant-one}\n\n\\uses{def:warped-product, def:product-metric}\n\n\n\n\nIf $f\\equiv1$, the warped product metric is $g_1\\oplus g_2$.\n\\end{proposition}\n\n\\begin{proof}\n\n\\uses{def:warped-product,def:product-metric}\n\\sketch Substituting $f=1$ into the defining bilinear form gives (2.12) at every point, hence the metrics coincide.\n\\end{proof}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Proposition</div></section>[Constant warping gives the product metric] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-70726f703a7761727065642d70726f647563742d636f6e7374616e742d6f6e65\"></span> <p></p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FLeeRiemannian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2Fchapter2%23def%3Awarped-product\">↗ def:warped-product</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FLeeRiemannian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2Fchapter2%23def%3Aproduct-metric\">↗ def:product-metric</a> <p></p>If <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$f\\equiv 1$\">$f\\equiv 1$</span>, the warped product metric is <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$g_1\\oplus g_2$\">$g_1\\oplus g_2$</span>.  <p></p><section class=\"theorem proof\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Proof</div><p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FLeeRiemannian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2Fchapter2%23def%3Awarped-product\">↗ def:warped-product</a> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FLeeRiemannian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2Fchapter2%23def%3Aproduct-metric\">↗ def:product-metric</a> \\sketch Substituting <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$f=1$\">$f=1$</span> into the defining bilinear form gives (2.12) at every point, hence the metrics coincide. </p><span class=\"qed\" aria-label=\"证毕\" style=\"display:block;text-align:right\">□</span></section>","warnings":["部分 TeX 排版细节未完整还原（已保留可读内容）: sketch"]},"previous":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:warped-product","next":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#ex:warped-products","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:warped-product","title":"def:warped-product","label":"def:warped-product"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:product-metric","title":"def:product-metric","label":"def:product-metric"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#ex:warped-products","title":"ex:warped-products","label":"ex:warped-products"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:warped-product-isometry","title":"prop:warped-product-isometry","label":"prop:warped-product-isometry"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prob:warped-product-isometry","title":"prob:warped-product-isometry","label":"prob:warped-product-isometry"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#prop:warped-product-constant-one","title":"prop:warped-product-constant-one","tex":"\\begin{proposition}[Constant warping gives the product metric]\n\\label{prop:warped-product-constant-one}\n\n\\uses{def:warped-product, def:product-metric}\n\n\n\n\nIf $f\\equiv1$, the warped product metric is $g_1\\oplus g_2$.\n\\end{proposition}\n\n\\begin{proof}\n\n\\uses{def:warped-product,def:product-metric}\n\\sketch Substituting $f=1$ into the defining bilinear form gives (2.12) at every point, hence the metrics coincide.\n\\end{proof}","source":"tex/references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2.tex","description":"","dependencies":[{"node":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:warped-product","title":"def:warped-product","description":""},{"node":"references/LeeRiemannian/blueprint/src/chapters/chapter2#def:product-metric","title":"def:product-metric","description":""}],"dependencies_omitted":0,"unresolved_dependencies":[],"unresolved_omitted":0}}